NEW = [
    ("p:incumbent-start", "A design passing a direction poll becomes the incumbent, and a rival without a paper cannot block it, though the chair may delay it one meeting for one to arrive. A rival with a paper can be polled alongside it, the room can direct changes, and by default a TS merge proceeds with it unless an alternative is actively progressing.", "'can at most delay it one meeting' is 'may delay it one meeting'"),
    ("p:review-share", "A longer, more complex design costs more to study and has more places where a defect can go unnoticed. Yet a reviewer whose stake does not grow with its size does better studying it less closely than a smaller design with the same stake.", "the dropped 'so ... gets less study' restated the two sentences; 'Yet' carries it"),
    ("p:light-review", "Participants who do not study a proposal typically abstain, so one costing more to study than most stakes in it is voted on mainly by those with large stakes. Unless those of them who object cast at least a third of the Favor-plus-Against votes, it can advance with no change at all.", "'typically do not vote' is 'typically abstain', which this proof uses for not voting"),
    ("p:burden-flip", "A design normally needs the 2:1 norm to advance, and once adopted, a paper or ballot comment to remove it normally needs the same, switching the default to keeping it. After a successful final ballot, design changes are expected to have near-unanimous consent, ISO bars technical changes at approval, and SD-4 calls published features much harder to update incompatibly.", "'breaking changes' is 'update incompatibly'"),
    ("p:late-arrival", "An objector who arrives after a design has been adopted faces three obstacles at once. Removal needs a paper meeting the 2:1 norm, reversal costs more for every decision built on the design, and the record keeps its case but unwritten objections only in memory.", ""),
    ("p:sponsor-features", "Changing the features a sponsor's interests depend on costs that sponsor rework of what it built on them, lost fit with its requirements, and more review, all still to come. The gain of a better design is spread across all the feature's users, and the same 2:1 norm decides every proposal, however many users will rely on it.", ""),
    ("p:parts", "Presenting a design in parts lets the first parts be adopted before the whole is reviewed, and each adopted part adds to what a rival to the whole must replace. The cost is a paper and a presentation for each part.", "'as a sequence of papers' is 'in parts', each with its own paper as the second sentence says"),
    ("p:first-part", "Reviewers cannot study what later parts need of a first part that fixes a design's structure, SD-4 asks no paper to say, and once adopted, every rival must replace it. So a design-bound author's best first paper is the smallest part that fixes that structure and still meets the 2:1 norm against opponents who foresee the rest.", ""),
    ("p:large-position", "A large proposal has more sponsored authors in the room than a small one, and more meetings where a poll can make it the incumbent or end that standing. Each step it advances brings it nearer to work that can make plenary progress, which SD-4 has chairs take up first.", "'authors with their costs paid' is 'sponsored authors'"),
    ("p:size-attributes", "Each result about large or small proposals rests on an attribute their definitions name: who pays the authors' costs, the number of authors, review's expected span, or the paper's length. So it holds for any proposal with that attribute, whatever its size.", ""),
]
